Muramasa Hellblade

An accursed weapon imbued with hatred for the Tokugawa Shoguns and all
those pledged to serve them (in game terms, all lawful samurai).

Intelligent chaotic artifact. Will NOT evade grasp of lawful samurai,
for reasons to be enumerated...

appearance: Katana with fish-shaped tang (unique to the Muramasa
blades).

Weapon appears only in Samurai games (in quest?)

Weapon is generated uncursed.  It's effects are more insidious ...

+0 weapon has intrinsic +4 to hit, +4 damage (reflecting the superb
craftsmanship of the Muramasa).  Magical bonuses add to this, so that a
+3 Muramasa has +7 hit, +7 damage.
10% chance instakill (twice that of the Tsurugi)
100% chance instakill if target is lawful samurai.
-- acts as blessed luckstone to chaotic character,  no effect for
neutral.

Quite a weapon, eh?  Not one to just throw away... but it has a price
...

-- causes conflict, if wielded or in main inventory (ensures that any
samurai will attack the wielder on sight).

-- attack without warning (like Stormbringer -- it's a really evil
weapon).

-- for any lawful character, acts as doubly cursed luckstone (e.g,
instead of losing 1 luck point every X turns, lose 1 luck point every
X/2 turns) if in main inventory or equipped (but sacks are okay).  This
effect overrides any other luck-generating device.

-- for any lawful SAMURAI, luck is further maxed out at -1.  (E.g., no
sacrifice, nothing you do, will ever get your luck above -1. If your
luck is above -1, it will be auto-set to -1 ... and after you get rid
of the weapon, you'll have to earn luck back the HARD WAY).

-- If lawful character, patron deity is permanently angry as long as
the weapon is in main inventory.  Reconciliation not possible.  Every
level is Gehenna to the wielder until it is dropped.

The idea, of course, is to tempt the user with a wonderful weapon that
seems the answer to all his prayers ..and not even realize that the run
of bad luck he's experiencing is more than just the RNG after him.   To
put him in a bad position from which he may try to escape through
prayer -- and discover that prayers will not work.  The blade that
seems so wonderful turns out to be his enemy, and betrays him in the
end.  Such is the fate of a lawful samurai who dares wield the cursed
blade.

Other effects:
Lawful characters receive bonus from Deity for destroying blade, either
through sacrifice or over-enchanting (a wish; if this is refused, an
artifact, spellbook, multiple points of protection, something like
that).

-- If the weapon is brought into the presence of the Samurai Quest
Leader, a dialog will come up, asking the Samurai to surrender the
terrible cursed weapon (which will not be named, but described so
vividly the player will have no doubt what he is talking about).

If the weapon is surrendered (for destruction),  the Samurai Quest
Leader will, on behalf of the Patron Deity, grant a Wish.

If the choice is refused, Quest Leader and all henchmen become
permanently angry.   Battle commences.

--Killing the Quest Leader (who should be a Tokugawa) with the Muramasa
results in:
	-- Instant conversion to chaotic alignment (with attendent penalties)
	-- Major benefit from Chaotic deity (artifact, multiple XP level
gains,  multiple protection points)
	-- Summons major demon, who then goes to town on the henchmen (if any
are left alive).

